          "tool_use_id": "toolu_01AkGLhAsHByQ68YMU9Qxf4y",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022: \u0022concepts/log-metric-correlation.md\u0022, \u0022content\u0022: \u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Log and Metric Correlation\\ndescription: Propagating trace context (trace_id and span_id) into logs and metrics to enable operators to pivot from observed anomalies to their underlying causes.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Observability\\n  - concept/Correlation\\n  - tech/OpenTelemetry\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\\u0022Single authoritative source (OpenTelemetry specification); correlates with established observability practices.\\\u0022\\nresource: https://opentelemetry.io/docs/concepts/signals/\\n---\\n\\nLog and metric correlation is the practice of embedding trace context (trace_id and span_id) into both log records and metric exemplars. This enables an operator observing a metric spike or anomaly to pivot directly to the specific distributed traces that caused it.\\n\\n## Mechanism\\n\\nThe correlation works by propagating a **trace_id** and **span_id** from the active span into:\\n\\n1. **Log records**: Each structured log event carries the trace/span IDs of the context in which it was emitted, making it possible to query all logs that belong to a single trace or span.\\n2. **Metrics exemplars**: Individual metric data points (especially on latency or error-rate metrics) can be annotated with the trace/span IDs of a representative request or transaction that contributed to that measurement.\\n\\nWhen the propagation is in place, an operator can observe a metric\u2014say, a spike in p99 latency or an elevated error rate\u2014then use the exemplar\u0027s embedded trace_id to jump directly to the full causal chain of spans and logs that produced that metric value. This drastically reduces mean time to investigation (MTTI).\\n\\n## Prerequisite: Trace Context\\n\\nCorrelation requires that trace_id and span_id are already being generated and propagated through the instrumented system as spans execute. The correlation layer then reuses that same context.\\n\\n## Related Concepts\\n\\n- [[concepts/distributed-tracing]] (if created in future)\\n- OpenTelemetry [[tech/OpenTelemetry]] (the standard that formalizes these signals and their correlation)\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: [OpenTelemetry Signals documentation](https://opentelemetry.io/docs/concepts/signals/)\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
